window.docContents[170].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-vector-spaces/#Chapter2___Definition2___11___1":{"contents":"Definition 2.11.1. The tensor product V \\otimes W of vector spaces V and W over a field k is the quotient of the space V * W whose basis is given by formal symbols v \\otimes w, v \\in V, w \\in W, by the subspace spanned by the elements\n\n(v_1 + v_2) \\otimes w - v_1 \\otimes w - v_2 \\otimes w,\nv \\otimes (w_1 + w_2) - v \\otimes w_1 - v \\otimes w_2,\nav \\otimes w - a(v \\otimes w),\nv \\otimes aw - a(v \\otimes w),\n\nwhere v \\in V, w \\in W, a \\in k.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products\u0009Tensor product of vector spaces","header":"Tensor product of vector spaces","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-vector-spaces/#Chapter2___Definition2___11___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma--Schur___s-lemma--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A nonzero linear map between simple modules is bijective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Proposition2.3.9; role=primary]\n\nA nonzero linear map out of a simple module is injective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Proposition2.3.9; role=primary]\n\nA nonzero linear map into a simple module is surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Proposition2.3.9; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Schur's lemma\u0009Schur's lemma\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Schur___s-lemma/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Schur___s-lemma--Schur___s-lemma--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___8___6___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Generators and relations for the path algebra\u0009Generators and relations for the path algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Generators-and-relations-for-the-path-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___8___6___formalization"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A--Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: Module.finrank_linearMap\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.5.5/Derived2; role=supporting\n\nUnder the displayed finite-dimensional simple-module hypotheses, the indicated quotient algebra is equivalent to a product of endomorphism algebras.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.5.5/Derived2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Theorem3.5.4; role=primary]\n\nFor a finite family of pairwise nonisomorphic finite-dimensional simple modules, the sum of the squares of their ranks is at most the rank of the algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.5.5; role=supporting]\n\nDeclaration: Submodule.finrank_quotient_add_finrank\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter3/Corollary3.5.5/Derived2; role=supporting\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Sum of squares of dimensions of irreducibles bounded by dim A\u0009Sum of squares of dimensions of irreducibles bounded by dim A\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras--Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A--Sum-of-squares-of-dimensions-of-irreducibles-bounded-by-dim-A--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers--Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary linear equivalence from the displayed partition-dependent space to a direct sum indexed by partitions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Proposition5.14.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.14.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe complex finrank of linear maps from the partition-indexed type to the indicated membership subtype is one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Proposition5.14.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.14.1; role=supporting]\n\nWhen the target partition is lexicographically smaller than the source partition, every linear map into its membership subtype is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.14.1; role=supporting]\n\nIf the partition relation fails in the reverse direction, every linear map into the corresponding membership subtype is zero.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Proposition5.14.1; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.14.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Decomposition of U_lambda in terms of V_mu with Kostka numbers\u0009Decomposition of U_lambda in terms of V_mu with Kostka numbers\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___--Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers--Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Chapter7___Example7___9___5":{"contents":"Example 7.9.5. The category of representations of a finite group G over a field of characteristic not dividing |G| (or 0) is semisimple.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Representations of finite groups as semisimple category","header":"Representations of finite groups as semisimple category","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Chapter7___Example7___9___5"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Section-7___9___-Exact-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Section 7.9: Exact functors","header":"Section 7.9: Exact functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Section-7___9___-Exact-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___9"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Chapter9___Problem9___5___3":{"contents":"Problem 9.5.3. (i) Show that there is a natural bijection between blocks of \\mathcal{C} and indecomposable central idempotents e_k of A (i.e., ones that cannot be nontrivially split in a sum of two central idempotents), such that \\mathcal{C}_k is the category of finite dimensional e_k A-modules.\n\n(ii) Show that any indecomposable object of \\mathcal{C} lies in some \\mathcal{C}_k and that \\operatorname{Hom}(M, N) = 0 if M \\in \\mathcal{C}_k, N \\in \\mathcal{C}_l, k \\neq l. Thus, \\mathcal{C} = \\bigoplus_{k \\in B} \\mathcal{C}_k.\n\n(iii) Determine the blocks in the category of left A-modules for A = k[S_3], where k is of characteristic 2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.5. Blocks\u0009Blocks and indecomposable central idempotents","header":"Blocks and indecomposable central idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Chapter9___Problem9___5___3"}});